{
  "concept": "Titrating alanine with hydroxide converts it stepwise from a fully protonated cation through the neutral zwitterion to the carboxylate anion, with the pKa values at half-equivalence points and the isoelectric point at one full equivalent.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Pale blue tinted plot area with light gray gridlines",
    "Vertical axis labeled 'pH', range 0 to 12, labeled ticks every 2 units",
    "Horizontal axis labeled 'Equivalents of HO⁻', range 0.0 to 2.0, labeled ticks every 0.5",
    "Magenta titration curve rising in two sigmoidal legs: shallow buffering near pH 2.34, near-vertical jump at 1.0 equivalent, second buffering plateau near pH 9.69, steep rise toward pH 11.5 at 2.0 equivalents",
    "Black 'x' marker at (0.5, 2.34) with annotation 'pKa1 = 2.34' and curved leader arrow",
    "Black 'x' marker at (1.0, 6.01) with annotation 'Isoelectric point = 6.01' and curved leader arrow",
    "Black 'x' marker at (1.5, 9.69) with annotation 'pKa2 = 9.69' and curved leader arrow",
    "Movable circular marker on the curve tied to the equivalents slider, with pH readout callout",
    "Structure panel at right: bracketed fully protonated cation H3N⁺-CH(CH3)-COOH (ammonium N in magenta, acidic OH in magenta)",
    "Structure panel: bracketed 50:50 pair at pKa1 — cation + zwitterion, joined by '+'",
    "Structure panel: bracketed zwitterion H3N⁺-CH(CH3)-COO⁻ alone",
    "Structure panel: bracketed 50:50 pair at pKa2 — zwitterion + anion, joined by '+'",
    "Structure panel: bracketed fully deprotonated anion H2N-CH(CH3)-COO⁻ (neutral amine N in blue)",
    "Thin black leader arrows from five curve positions (0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0 equivalents) to their structures",
    "Net-charge overlay badges (+1, 0, −1) and colored charge markers usable in the charge view",
    "Legend/key noting magenta = protonated ammonium nitrogen, blue = neutral amine nitrogen"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "equivalents",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Equivalents of HO⁻ added",
      "range": [0, 2, 0.05],
      "default": 0,
      "teaches": "How pH responds unevenly to added base — flat buffering regions around each pKa and a sharp jump at the equivalence point — and which alanine species dominates at each stage."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Right-panel view",
      "options": ["Species structures", "Net charge"],
      "default": "Species structures",
      "teaches": "That the zwitterion, though it carries both a + and a − site, has zero net charge, which is exactly why pH 6.01 is the isoelectric point."
    },
    {
      "id": "landmark",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "Clicking pKa1, the isoelectric point, or pKa2 shows that half-equivalence points hold a 50:50 mixture of two species (pH = pKa) while the full equivalence point holds essentially a single neutral zwitterion."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Start: fully protonated cation",
      "narration": "With no base added, alanine sits at pH ~0.3 as the cation: protonated ammonium and intact carboxylic acid, net charge +1.",
      "state": { "equivalents": 0, "viewMode": "Species structures" }
    },
    {
      "title": "First buffering region: pKa1",
      "narration": "At 0.5 equivalent the carboxylic acid is half neutralized; the solution holds equal amounts of cation and zwitterion and the pH equals pKa1 = 2.34.",
      "state": { "equivalents": 0.5, "viewMode": "Species structures" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Equivalence point: the zwitterion",
      "narration": "One full equivalent converts nearly all the COOH to COO⁻. The pH leaps through the steep region and settles at 6.01, where the zwitterion dominates.",
      "state": { "equivalents": 1.0, "viewMode": "Species structures" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Why 6.01 is the isoelectric point",
      "narration": "Switch to the net-charge view: the +1 ammonium and −1 carboxylate cancel, so the dominant species carries zero net charge and will not migrate in an electric field.",
      "state": { "equivalents": 1.0, "viewMode": "Net charge" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Second buffering region: pKa2",
      "narration": "At 1.5 equivalents the ammonium group is half deprotonated; zwitterion and anion are present 50:50 and pH = pKa2 = 9.69.",
      "state": { "equivalents": 1.5, "viewMode": "Species structures" }
    },
    {
      "title": "End: fully deprotonated anion",
      "narration": "By 2.0 equivalents both acidic protons are gone, leaving the neutral blue amine and carboxylate anion with net charge −1 near pH 11.5.",
      "state": { "equivalents": 2.0, "viewMode": "Net charge" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Each flat stretch of the curve is a 50:50 buffer where pH equals a pKa, and the isoelectric point sits midway between the two pKa values — the one pH where alanine exists almost entirely as the zero-net-charge zwitterion.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout: titration plot occupies the left ~45% of the canvas, structure panel the right ~55%. Plot area filled pale blue (#ddeffa) with thin gray gridlines at every labeled tick; axes black with 'pH' rotated at left (0–12, ticks every 2) and 'Equivalents of HO⁻' beneath (0.0–2.0, ticks every 0.5). Curve is a thick magenta/crimson (#e8186c) double-sigmoid: rises steeply from pH ~0.3 at zero equivalents, flattens through pH 2.34 near 0.5 eq, bends upward and jumps near-vertically at 1.0 eq passing through pH 6.01, flattens again through 9.69 at 1.5 eq, then climbs to ~11.5 at 2.0 eq. Three black 'x' markers with small curved leader arrows and labels 'pKa1 = 2.34', 'Isoelectric point = 6.01', 'pKa2 = 9.69'. Right panel stacks five bracketed structural formulas top-to-bottom in the order anion, zwitterion+anion pair, zwitterion, cation+zwitterion pair, cation — each connected by a thin black arrow to its matching point on the curve. Draw skeletal condensed formulas: H3N⁺ in magenta for protonated nitrogen, H2N in blue for neutral amine, CH3 on a vertical bond below the alpha carbon, C=O with O above and O⁻ or OH to the right (acidic H in magenta). In net-charge view, replace/overlay each structure with large colored charge badges (+1 red, 0 gray, −1 blue) and tint the charged sites. Active slider position shows a filled dot on the curve plus a dashed drop-line to both axes with a live pH readout; clicked landmarks glow with a highlight ring and their structure panel gets a highlighted border while others dim."
}

